= Solution
{parent=i}

**False.** Take $A=\{0\}$ and $B=C=\{0,1\}$, with $f(0)=0$ and $g=\operatorname{id}_B$. Then $f$ is an <injection> and $g$ is a <surjection>, but the <function composition> $g\circ f$ has <image of a function> $\{0\}$, so it is not a <surjection> onto $C$.

= ii
{parent=1d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

**True.** The <function composition> $j=g\circ f$ is an <injection>: $j(a)=j(a')$ first implies $f(a)=f(a')$ by the <injectivity> of $g$, then $a=a'$ by the <injectivity> of $f$. Fix one $a_0\in A$, using the given nonemptiness, and define
$$
h(c)=\begin{cases}a,&c=j(a)\text{ for some }a\in A,\\a_0,&c\notin j(A).\end{cases}
$$
The first case is well defined because $j$ is an <injection>. For every $a\in A$, $h(j(a))=a$. Thus this <left inverse> satisfies $\boxed{h\circ g\circ f=\operatorname{id}_A}$, the <identity function> on $A$.

= iii
{parent=1d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

**False.** Take $A=\{0,1\}$, $B=\{0\}$ and the <constant function> $f(a)=0$. For the <subsets> $X=\{0\}$ and $Y=\{1\}$, the <intersection> $X\cap Y$ is empty, so $f(X\cap Y)=\varnothing$, whereas the <intersection> of their <images of a function> is $f(X)\cap f(Y)=\{0\}$. In general the inclusion $f(X\cap Y)\subseteq f(X)\cap f(Y)$ holds, but a common image value can arise from different points of $X$ and $Y$.

= iv
{parent=1d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

**True.** For each $a\in A$, the definition of <preimage> gives
$$
a\in f^{-1}(Z\cap W)\iff f(a)\in Z\cap W\iff f(a)\in Z\text{ and }f(a)\in W\iff a\in f^{-1}(Z)\cap f^{-1}(W).
$$
Thus $\boxed{f^{-1}(Z\cap W)=f^{-1}(Z)\cap f^{-1}(W)}$. Unlike the corresponding statement for <images of a function>, this <intersection> identity requires no <injectivity>.

